/**
 * @defgroup Foundation_Workflows_Precompiled Precompiled Libraries
 * @ingroup Foundation_Workflows_Delivery
 * @brief Compatibility and consumption rules for exported static libraries.
 *
 * Precompiled libraries reduce build time for projects that consume Foundation,
 * while the repository remains the source of truth. An exported library is only
 * compatible with the operating system, compiler/toolchain, architecture, ABI,
 * build configuration, and compiled feature macros used to create it.
 *
 * A Windows MSVC `.lib` is not assumed to be compatible with MinGW. An Arm GNU
 * Cortex-M3 soft-float `.a` is not compatible with a Cortex-M4 hard-float target.
 * Keep each target package in its own `dist/<preset>` directory; its Debug and
 * Release configurations may coexist there under distinct library names.
 *
 * @section FoundationPrecompiledCompatibilityMatrix Compatibility Matrix
 *
 * Rebuild or select another package whenever one of these changes:
 *
 * | Dimension | Examples | Why it matters |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | operating system/object format | Windows COFF, macOS Mach-O, ELF | Linkers consume different formats. |
 * | compiler/toolchain | MSVC, Apple Clang, Arm GNU, AVR-GCC | Name mangling, runtime, and flags may differ. |
 * | architecture/core | x86-64, arm64, Cortex-M3, AVR5 | Machine instructions are target-specific. |
 * | ABI/FPU | Arm soft float, M4F hard float | Function calling conventions differ. |
 * | C++ runtime policy | exceptions/RTTI enabled or disabled | Runtime symbols and object assumptions differ. |
 * | feature options | Scheduling on/off | Headers, macros, and compiled symbols differ. |
 * | configuration | Debug, Release | Debug postfix and optimization/debug information differ. |
 *
 * Debug and Release are handled inside one package. Every other incompatible
 * dimension belongs in the preset identity or in a deliberately isolated custom
 * feature package.
 *
 * @section FoundationPrecompiledFullBuild Full Build
 *
 * A full build enables all Foundation modules. It is useful for desktop
 * integration, testing, examples, and quick prototyping.
 *
 * @section FoundationPrecompiledCustomBuild Custom Build
 *
 * A custom build enables only the modules required by a product. It is useful
 * for embedded systems, memory-sensitive projects, and production firmware.
 * Configure feature options before exporting and preserve the preset identity
 * with the resulting package.
 *
 * The feature set is part of package compatibility even though it is not part of
 * the normal preset name. Store custom variants in separate distribution paths,
 * retain their `FoundationBuildInfo.cmake`, and never overlay two variants.
 *
 * @section FoundationPrecompiledRecommendedFlow Recommended Flow
 *
 * @code{bash}
 * ./scripts/configure.sh <preset> --fresh
 * ./scripts/test.sh <preset> --config Debug
 * ./scripts/export.sh <preset>
 * @endcode
 *
 * On Windows:
 *
 * @code{powershell}
 * ./scripts/configure.ps1 <preset> -Fresh
 * ./scripts/test.ps1 <preset> -Configuration Debug
 * ./scripts/export.ps1 <preset>
 * @endcode
 *
 * The current test suite is empty; see @ref Foundation_Workflows_Test before
 * treating the test step above as a release gate. For cross-compiled targets,
 * replace host CTest execution with firmware/consumer validation described in
 * @ref Foundation_Workflows_Validation.
 *
 * @section FoundationPrecompiledConsumer Consumer Contract
 *
 * A CMake consumer should use:
 *
 * @code{cmake}
 * find_package(Foundation 1.0 CONFIG REQUIRED)
 * target_link_libraries(MyTarget PRIVATE Foundation::Foundation)
 * @endcode
 *
 * The imported target carries include directories, compile features, public
 * definitions, and Debug/Release archive mappings. Consumers must not copy only
 * the archive and omit the installed headers/package metadata.
 *
 * See @ref Foundation_Workflows_Export for package contents and
 * `find_package(Foundation CONFIG)` integration.
 */
